# SYNOPSIS

```c
vx_status vx_counter_create(uint64_t initial, vx_handle *out);
vx_status vx_counter_signal(vx_handle c, uint64_t value);
int64_t vx_counter_read(vx_handle c);
```

# DESCRIPTION

A counter is a kernel object holding a 64-bit value that never goes down (see the kernel's design, 01 §4.4).
It is a timeline: a producer raises it as work completes, and anyone holding a handle waits for it to reach a value by binding it to a port with `VX_TRIGGER_COUNTER_GE` (see port(2)).
Ring doorbells are counters, and GPU fences are to be.

`vx_counter_create` makes a counter holding <initial> and stores a handle to it in <*out>.
The handle carries `VX_RIGHT_READ`, `VX_RIGHT_SIGNAL`, `VX_RIGHT_WAIT`, `VX_RIGHT_DUPLICATE`, `VX_RIGHT_TRANSFER` and `VX_RIGHT_INSPECT`.
A handle duplicated with `VX_RIGHT_READ` and `VX_RIGHT_WAIT` alone lets its holder watch the counter but not move it.

`vx_counter_signal` sets the counter to the larger of its value and <value>.
If that raises it, every `VX_TRIGGER_COUNTER_GE` binding whose threshold the new value reaches fires, carrying the new value.
A <value> at or below the current one changes nothing and still succeeds.
<c> needs `VX_RIGHT_SIGNAL`.

`vx_counter_read` returns the counter's value.
<c> may also be a ring end, whose own doorbell it reads (see the kernel's design, 01 §4.3).
<c> needs `VX_RIGHT_READ`.

The counter is destroyed with its last handle, and its unfired bindings with it.

# EXAMPLES

From the kernel's tests: a binding fires when the counter reaches its threshold, and the counter never goes back.

```c
  CHECK(vx_counter_create(0, &c) == VX_OK);
  CHECK(vx_port_bind(port, c, VX_TRIGGER_COUNTER_GE, 14, 5) == VX_OK);
  CHECK(vx_counter_signal(c, 3) == VX_OK);
  CHECK(vx_port_wait(port, after_ms(1), 0, pk, 4) == VX_ERR_TIMED_OUT);
  CHECK(vx_counter_signal(c, 7) == VX_OK);
  CHECK(vx_port_wait(port, after_ms(100), 0, pk, 4) == 1 && pk[0].key == 14 &&
        pk[0].value == 7);
  CHECK(vx_counter_signal(c, 6) == VX_OK && vx_counter_read(c) == 7);
```

